/**
@mainpage LifeLua Documentation

LifeLua is a Lua interpreter for the PS Vita, based on LuaJIT 2.1. You write your app in Lua and LifeLua runs it,
with libraries for drawing, images, sound, controls, files, networking, system dialogs and more.

## Getting started ##

1. Install the LifeLua VPK on your PS Vita.
2. Write your app in a file called `main.lua`.
3. Put `main.lua` in LifeLua's app folder, `ux0:app/LIFELUAJT/` (that's `app0:` from inside the app),
   together with any images, sounds or fonts it uses.
4. Start LifeLua from the LiveArea. It runs `main.lua`.

If `main.lua` is missing or has an error, LifeLua shows the error message on screen with three options:
press **Cross** to run `main.lua` again, **Square** to start an FTP server (so you can upload a fixed `main.lua`
from a computer without leaving the app) or **Circle** to close LifeLua. This makes it quick to fix mistakes and try again.

## Your first app ##

A LifeLua app is usually one loop that runs once per frame: read the controls, draw everything, then show the frame.

@code
local white = color.new(255, 255, 255)
local x, y = 480, 272

while true do
    controls.update()                          -- read the buttons

    if controls.check(SCE_CTRL_UP) then y = y - 4 end
    if controls.check(SCE_CTRL_DOWN) then y = y + 4 end
    if controls.check(SCE_CTRL_LEFT) then x = x - 4 end
    if controls.check(SCE_CTRL_RIGHT) then x = x + 4 end
    if controls.pressed(SCE_CTRL_START) then os.exit() end

    draw.text(10, 10, "Move the circle with the D-pad, press Start to exit", white)
    draw.circle(x, y, 20, white)

    draw.swapbuffers()                         -- show the frame
end
@endcode

More examples are in the [Examples](examples.html) tab, and the `sample` folder of the
[source code](https://github.com/HarommelRabbid/LifeLua/tree/main/sample) has a full showcase app.

## How to read this documentation ##

The libraries are listed in the [Libraries](topics.html) tab. Every function is shown like this:

@code{.c}
nil image.display(image image, number x, number y, color tint)
@endcode

- The first word is what the function **returns**. `nil` means it returns nothing, `any` means the type can vary.
- The words before each parameter name are the Lua **types** it expects: `number`, `string`, `boolean`, `table`, `function`,
  or one of LifeLua's own types like `image` or `color`.
- Parameters marked *(optional)* can be left out, starting from the end. Their default value is written next to them.
- Names in CAPITALS (like `SCE_CTRL_CROSS`) are **constants**: global variables holding numbers, ready to use.
- Functions whose first parameter is an object (an image, a color, an audio, ...) can also be called as **methods**,
  so `image.display(img, 0, 0)` can be written as `img:display(0, 0)`.

## Libraries ##

| Library | What it's for |
|---------|---------------|
| @ref draw "draw" | Drawing text and shapes, showing each frame |
| @ref color "color" | Creating and mixing colors |
| @ref image "image" | Loading, drawing, editing and saving images, QR codes |
| @ref font "font" | Loading fonts for text |
| @ref controls "controls" | Buttons, analog sticks, touch screens, motion sensors |
| @ref audio "audio" | Music and sounds |
| @ref video "video" | Playing videos |
| @ref camera "camera" | The front and back cameras |
| @ref imgui "imgui" | Windows, menus, buttons and other UI widgets |
| @ref timer "timer" | Measuring time |
| @ref os "os" | System dialogs (keyboard, messages), apps, battery, power, system info |
| @ref io "io" | Files, folders, archives, hashes |
| @ref network "network" | Wi-Fi, downloads, FTP server |
| @ref json "json" / @ref toml "toml" | Reading and writing JSON, reading TOML |
| @ref sqlite3 "sqlite3" | SQLite databases |
| @ref thread "thread" | Running code at the same time as your main loop |
| @ref string "string" / @ref table "table" / @ref math "math" | Extra functions added to Lua's standard libraries |

All of the standard Lua 5.1 libraries are available too (`string`, `table`, `math`, `io`, `os`, ...),
plus LuaJIT's `bit`, `ffi` and `jit` libraries. They are documented in the [Lua 5.1 manual](https://www.lua.org/manual/5.1/manual.html)
and on the [LuaJIT website](https://luajit.org/extensions.html).
The [luautf8](https://github.com/starwing/luautf8) library by starwing is also included, as `utf8`, for working with UTF-8 text.

## LifeLua's types ##

Besides Lua's own types, LifeLua functions use these objects:

| Type | Made with | Represents |
|------|-----------|------------|
| `color` | colorꓸnew(), tocolor() | A color with red, green, blue and alpha |
| `image` | imageꓸload(), imageꓸnew(), imageꓸscreen(), imageꓸqr() | An image that can be drawn on the screen |
| `font` | fontꓸload() | A font for drawꓸtext() |
| `audio` | audioꓸload() | A sound or a song |
| `timer` | timerꓸnew() | A stopwatch |
| `thread` | threadꓸnew() | A function running at the same time as the main code |
| `sqlite3` | sqlite3ꓸopen() | An open database |
| `jsonnull` | json.null | JSON's `null` value |

They are freed automatically when they're no longer used, like any other Lua value.

## Callbacks ##

Some functions wait until they're done (like a download or a system dialog), so your main loop doesn't run in the meantime.
To show progress or keep drawing during that time, define a **global function** with one of these names. LifeLua calls it for you:

| Function name | Called during | Parameters |
|---------------|---------------|------------|
| `LifeLuaNetworkDownload` | networkꓸdownload() | downloaded bytes, written bytes, total size, piece size, written piece size |
| `LifeLuaArchiveExtracting` | ioꓸextract() | file name, file number, file size |
| `LifeLuaIMEDialog`, `LifeLuaMessageDialog`, ... | The @ref os "os" dialogs | none (see the list in @ref os "os") |

## Tips ##

- `print()` writes to the debug console, which isn't visible on the PS Vita's screen. Use drawꓸtext() to show values while testing.
- Load images, sounds and fonts **once**, before your main loop, not inside it: loading every frame is slow and wastes memory.
- Save your app's files in its own folder in `ux0:data/` (e.g. `ux0:data/myapp/`), and create it with ioꓸnewfolder() first.
- Lua lists start at index **1**, and LifeLua's lists (touch points, folder contents, combo box items, ...) do too.

## Compiling LifeLua ##

To get the newest features before a release, you can compile LifeLua yourself. See the
[README](https://github.com/HarommelRabbid/LifeLua#compiling) for the steps.

## About this documentation ##
This documentation was largely rewritten with the help of AI (Claude by Anthropic), checked against LifeLua's source code.
If you find something that doesn't match how LifeLua behaves, please [open an issue](https://github.com/HarommelRabbid/LifeLua/issues).

